    answer: "The answer: In most cases, yes. Nebraska requires plans for commercial, industrial, and multi-family buildings to bear the seal of a professional engineer licensed by the Nebraska Board of Engineers and Architects who is in responsible charge of the work. The seal certifies the engineer personally directed the design, and building departments in Omaha, Lincoln, and across the state check license status at plan intake.\\n\\nThe Nebraska Board of Engineers and Architects licenses individual engineers — there is no shortcut around the individual license. The stamping engineer must hold an active Nebraska PE, must have exercised responsible charge (direct supervision and control of the engineering decisions, not a rubber-stamp review of someone else's drawings), and the seal must show the engineer's name, Nebraska license number, and the date of sealing. Out-of-state engineers must obtain Nebraska licensure by comity before sealing Nebraska plans; Nebraska does offer comity for qualified engineers licensed elsewhere, but the stamp cannot go on Nebraska drawings until the Nebraska license is active.\\n\\nNebraska's engineering signature is written in wind and agriculture. The state sits in tornado country, and the structural design follows the wind speed maps with the detailing the exposure demands — the plan reviewer expects the design wind speed, exposure category, and lateral system stated on the structural drawings. Agricultural construction adds a Nebraska-specific layer: grain storage, livestock facilities, and equipment buildings are engineered for heavy imposed loads, corrosive environments, and wind uplift on large clear-span roofs. These structures fail in wind events when they are under-designed, so the jurisdictions that do require stamped ag plans take the structural review seriously.\\n\\nThe gray areas center on agricultural and small rural buildings. Nebraska's practice act exempts certain farm structures from the engineering licensure requirement, and many rural counties run lean building departments with limited plan review. But an exemption from the licensure law is not an exemption from physics — or from liability. Commercial occupancies, public assembly, and anything with unusual structural demands still need a Nebraska PE's stamp, and lenders and insurers routinely require stamped drawings even where the county does not. When in doubt, the stamped set is the cheaper risk.",
