    answer: "The answer: For commercial, institutional, industrial, and multi-family construction, yes. Oregon requires the seal of a professional engineer licensed by the Oregon State Board of Examiners for Engineering and Land Surveying who is in responsible charge of the work. The board's responsible-charge doctrine is strict — the sealing engineer must have exercised direct supervision and control over the engineering decisions — and Oregon's building departments verify the Oregon license at plan intake.\\n\\nThe Oregon State Board of Examiners for Engineering and Land Surveying licenses individual engineers, and the seal must show the engineer's name, Oregon license number, and the date of sealing. Oregon offers comity licensure for engineers licensed in other states, but Oregon plans cannot be sealed until the Oregon license is issued. Oregon also recognizes a Structural Engineer (SE) authority — the state's structural engineering practice for significant structures carries additional expectations, and the building official can require SE-level involvement on complex structural work.\\n\\nOregon's defining feature is the Oregon Structural Specialty Code — the statewide structural code (OSSC) that applies uniformly from Portland to Pendleton. Unlike states where every city amends the base code differently, Oregon's structural provisions are consistent statewide, which simplifies the engineer's code research but concentrates the reviewer's expertise: Oregon plan checkers know the OSSC cold. The Cascadia subduction zone shapes the seismic design for the entire western corridor — Portland, Salem, Eugene, and the coast sit in significant seismic design categories, and the structural drawings state the seismic parameters, the lateral system, and the detailing. Oregon's energy code adds a rigorous efficiency layer reviewed alongside the structural set.\\n\\nPermitting follows the site. Portland's Bureau of Development Services runs the state's largest and most demanding review, with design review overlays in many districts; Salem, Eugene, Bend, Medford, and Hillsboro run their own plan checks under the same statewide structural code. Eastern Oregon's high desert and the coastal communities apply the same OSSC with different environmental inputs — wind exposure on the coast, snow in the Cascades, wildland-urban interface in the east. The engineer confirms the authority having jurisdiction before design begins, because the review process and overlays vary even though the structural code does not.",
