    h1: "Do I Really Need an Engineer Stamp for My Hawaii Project?",
    answer: "The answer: For commercial, institutional, industrial, and multi-family construction, yes. Hawaii requires the seal of a professional engineer licensed by the Hawaii Board of Professional Engineers, Architects, Surveyors and Landscape Architects who is in responsible charge of the work. The board's responsible-charge standard ties the seal to genuine supervision of the engineering decisions, and Hawaii's county building departments verify the Hawaii license at plan intake.\\n\\nThe Hawaii Board of Professional Engineers, Architects, Surveyors and Landscape Architects licenses individual engineers, and the seal must show the engineer's name, Hawaii license number, and the date of sealing. Hawaii offers comity licensure for engineers licensed in other states, but Hawaii drawings cannot be sealed until the Hawaii license is issued. Hawaii also recognizes structural engineering as a distinct discipline — the structural design of significant structures carries additional expectations, and the county building official can require a structural engineer's involvement on complex work.\\n\\nHawaii's engineering is hurricane, volcano, and salt air. The islands sit in the highest wind speed regions of the U.S. building codes — the structural design states the hurricane wind speed, exposure category, and the continuous load path, and the reviewer checks the uplift connections with the seriousness the storm history demands. The Big Island adds volcanic hazard: lava flow zones shape where and how structures are built, and volcanic emissions corrode standard materials, pushing the design toward corrosion-resistant detailing. Salt-laden air attacks every island's structures, so the structural drawings address material protection and connection durability as a matter of course.\\n\\nPermitting in Hawaii runs county by county — there is no state building department. The City and County of Honolulu's Department of Planning and Permitting runs Oahu's review; Hawaii County, Maui County, and Kauai County each run their own with local amendments and, on the neighbor islands, distinct environmental and shoreline review layers. The same commercial building on Oahu versus Maui faces different submittal checklists, different reviewers, and different timelines. The engineer confirms the county authority having jurisdiction before design begins, because the amendments, the shoreline setbacks, and the review sequence all depend on it.",
    directAnswer: "Hawaii commercial, institutional, and multi-family projects require the seal of a Hawaii-licensed PE in responsible charge — and permitting runs county by county, not through the state. Hurricane wind design, Big Island lava zones, and salt-air corrosion define the islands' engineering.",
