        heading: "When Hawaii Requires an Engineer's Stamp",
        body: "Hawaii requires a licensed engineer's seal on plans for buildings where engineering affects public safety — commercial, institutional, industrial, and multi-family construction, plus structural, mechanical, and electrical systems beyond prescriptive residential scope. The Hawaii Board of Professional Engineers, Architects, Surveyors and Landscape Architects sets the trigger by the work, and the county building departments — Honolulu DPP, Hawaii County, Maui County, Kauai County — verify the Hawaii license number at submittal. A mainland seal on Hawaii plans stops the review before it starts.\\n\\nApex Grid Engineering assigns Hawaii-licensed engineers as engineers of record on Hawaii projects, so the stamp on your plans belongs to the licensed professional who directed the design. Island engineering punishes mainland assumptions — the hurricane wind speeds, the exposure categories, the corrosion environment, and the county-specific amendments are not the numbers the engineer used on the last mainland project. The seal should come from a Hawaii-licensed engineer who designed for the specific island and the specific county's review.",
