        heading: "Exemptions and Gray Areas in Hawaii",
        body: "Hawaii exempts single-family dwellings and certain agricultural buildings from the engineering licensure requirement. The gray areas follow the islands' tourism economy: single-family homes converted to vacation rentals with assembly-like occupancy loads, agricultural structures repurposed for events and weddings, and the additions and alterations that accumulate on older island buildings. Each can cross into work requiring a Hawaii PE's seal, and the counties — particularly Honolulu — scrutinize change-of-occupancy submittals closely.\\n\\nShoreline and environmental review is Hawaii's distinctive overlay. Construction near the shoreline faces setback requirements, and on some islands the erosion-based setbacks move the buildable area significantly landward from where the owner expected. Special management area permits layer environmental review over the building permit, with their own timelines. These approvals run parallel to structural plan check, and the engineer coordinates the structural design with the setback and environmental constraints from programming — discovering the shoreline setback at plan check can invalidate the structural scheme. The Big Island's lava zones add a further siting constraint the engineer and the county address together.",
